# Testing Guidelines

> **Note:** This document provides comprehensive instructions for AI agents working on the testing layer. Always refer to `/​.ai/​overall.mdc` for project-wide testing strategy and requirements.

## Testing Philosophy

All tests must be:

* **Comprehensive**: Cover happy paths, edge cases, and error scenarios
* **Independent**: Tests must not depend on execution order or shared state
* **Fast**: Optimize for quick execution (mock external dependencies)
* **Maintainable**: Clear, readable code with descriptive names
* **Reliable**: Tests should not be flaky or produce false positives

## Test Coverage Requirements

* **UI Layer (Jest)**: ≥90% coverage
* **API Layer (JUnit)**: ≥90% coverage
* **Integration Layer (Python)**: Critical paths must have end-to-end tests

## Test Types

### 1. Unit Tests

**Purpose**: Test individual components, functions, or methods in isolation

**Characteristics**:

* Fast execution (< 1 second per test)
* Mock all external dependencies
* Test one thing at a time
* No database, network, or file system access

### 2. Integration Tests

**Purpose**: Test interactions between components and external systems

**Characteristics**:

* Test complete workflows end-to-end
* Use real database (test database)
* Make actual HTTP requests
* Validate API contracts
* Test authentication flows

### 3. End-to-End Tests

**Purpose**: Test complete user journeys from UI to database

**Characteristics**:

* Simulate real user behavior
* Test critical business flows
* Use real or staging environment
* Validate UI, API, and database integration

### 4. Performance Tests

**Status**: Out of scope for now (can be added later)

## Unit Testing (API Layer - JUnit + Mockito)

### Test Class Structure

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {
    
    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;
    
    @Mock
    private PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;
    
    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;
    
    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        // Common setup for all tests
    }
    
    @Test
    @DisplayName("Should create user successfully when email is unique")
    void createUser_whenEmailIsUnique_shouldCreateSuccessfully() {
        // Arrange
        // Act
        // Assert
    }
}
```

### Naming Conventions

**Test Methods**: `methodName_whenCondition_shouldExpectedOutcome`

Examples:

* `createUser_whenEmailIsUnique_shouldCreateSuccessfully`
* `loginUser_whenPasswordIsInvalid_shouldThrowAuthenticationException`
* `deleteUser_whenUserNotFound_shouldThrowNotFoundException`

**DisplayName Requirement**: **All test methods MUST have `@DisplayName` annotations with clear, descriptive names**

```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Should return 404 when user ID does not exist")
void getUser_whenIdDoesNotExist_shouldReturn404() {
    // Test implementation
}
```

### Test Structure (AAA Pattern)

```java
@Test
@DisplayName("Should update user email when new email is valid and unique")
void updateUser_whenNewEmailIsValidAndUnique_shouldUpdateSuccessfully() {
    // Arrange - Set up test data and mocks
    UUID userId = UUID.randomUUID();
    UpdateUserRequest request = new UpdateUserRequest("newemail@example.com");
    User existingUser = User.builder()
        .id(userId)
        .email("oldemail@example.com")
        .build();
    
    when(userRepository.findById(userId)).thenReturn(Optional.of(existingUser));
    when(userRepository.existsByEmail(request.getEmail())).thenReturn(false);
    
    // Act - Execute the method under test
    ApiResponse<UserViewModel> response = userService.updateUserEmail(userId, request);
    
    // Assert - Verify the results
    assertTrue(response.isSuccess());
    assertEquals("newemail@example.com", response.getData().getEmail());
    verify(userRepository).save(any(User.class));
    verify(userRepository).findById(userId);
}
```

### What to Test in Each Layer

**Controller Tests**:

* Request validation (invalid inputs)
* Response status codes
* Response body structure
* Authentication/authorization
* Exception handling

**Service Tests**:

* Business logic correctness
* Edge cases and boundary conditions
* Error handling and exception throwing
* Interaction with repositories
* Transaction behavior

**Repository Tests**:

* Custom query methods
* Complex JOIN queries
* Native SQL queries
* (Standard Spring Data methods don't need tests)

### Mocking Guidelines

```java
// Mock repository responses
when(userRepository.findById(userId))
    .thenReturn(Optional.of(user));

when(userRepository.save(any(User.class)))
    .thenAnswer(invocation -> invocation.getArgument(0));

// Verify method calls
verify(userRepository).save(any(User.class));
verify(userRepository, times(1)).findById(userId);
verify(userRepository, never()).delete(any());

// Argument captors for complex verification
ArgumentCaptor<User> userCaptor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(User.class);
verify(userRepository).save(userCaptor.capture());
assertEquals("expected@email.com", userCaptor.getValue().getEmail());
```

## Unit Testing (UI Layer - Jest + React Testing Library)

### Test File Structure

```typescript
import { render, screen, fireEvent, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react-native';
import { UserProfileScreen } from '../UserProfileScreen';

// Mock dependencies
jest.mock('@react-native-async-storage/async-storage', () => ({
  __esModule: true,
  default: {
    getItem: jest.fn(),
    setItem: jest.fn(),
  },
}));

describe('UserProfileScreen', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    jest.clearAllMocks();
  });

  it('should display user name when data is loaded', async () => {
    // Test implementation
  });

  it('should show error message when fetch fails', async () => {
    // Test implementation
  });
});
```

### Naming Conventions

**Test Descriptions**: Use descriptive `it()` or `test()` descriptions

Examples:

* `'should display loading spinner while fetching data'`
* `'should navigate to login screen when user is not authenticated'`
* `'should show error message when email is invalid'`

### Testing User Interactions

```typescript
it('should submit form when user clicks submit button', async () => {
  // Arrange
  render(<LoginForm />);
  const emailInput = screen.getByTestId('email-input');
  const passwordInput = screen.getByTestId('password-input');
  const submitButton = screen.getByTestId('submit-button');

  // Act
  fireEvent.changeText(emailInput, 'test@example.com');
  fireEvent.changeText(passwordInput, 'password123');
  fireEvent.press(submitButton);

  // Assert
  await waitFor(() => {
    expect(screen.getByText('Login successful')).toBeTruthy();
  });
});
```

### Testing Async Operations

```typescript
it('should fetch and display user data on mount', async () => {
  // Mock API response
  const mockUser = { id: '123', name: 'John Doe', email: 'john@example.com' };
  jest.spyOn(UserService, 'getUser').mockResolvedValue({
    isSuccess: true,
    data: mockUser,
  });

  // Render component
  render(<UserProfile userId="123" />);

  // Wait for async operations
  await waitFor(() => {
    expect(screen.getByText('John Doe')).toBeTruthy();
    expect(screen.getByText('john@example.com')).toBeTruthy();
  });
});
```

### Test Data Queries

**Preferred**: Use `getByTestId` for reliable element selection

```typescript
const button = screen.getByTestId('submit-button');
const input = screen.getByTestId('email-input');
```

**Alternative** (when testId is not available):

```typescript
const button = screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' });
const heading = screen.getByText('Welcome');
```

## Integration Testing (Python + Pytest)

### Test File Structure

```python
import pytest
import requests
from typing import Dict, Any

BASE_URL = "http://localhost:9009/api/v1"

@pytest.fixture
def auth_headers() -> Dict[str, str]:
    """Get authentication headers with valid JWT token"""
    # Login and get token
    response = requests.post(f"{BASE_URL}/auth/login", json={
        "email": "test@example.com",
        "password": "password123"
    })
    token = response.json()["data"]["accessToken"]
    return {"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}"}

def test_create_user_success(auth_headers):
    """Test creating a new user with valid data"""
    # Arrange
    payload = {
        "email": "newuser@example.com",
        "name": "New User",
        "password": "SecurePass123!"
    }
    
    # Act
    response = requests.post(
        f"{BASE_URL}/users",
        json=payload,
        headers=auth_headers
    )
    
    # Assert
    assert response.status_code == 201
    data = response.json()
    assert data["isSuccess"] is True
    assert data["data"]["email"] == payload["email"]
```

### How to Define a New API Test

1. **Identify the Endpoint**: Determine URL, HTTP method, and authentication requirements
2. **Create Test Function**: Name it descriptively (e.g., `test_create_user_when_email_exists_returns_409`)
3. **Arrange**: Prepare test data, auth tokens, and preconditions
4. **Act**: Make the HTTP request
5. **Assert**: Verify response status, body structure, and data correctness

### Adding Tests to Flow

**Flow Definition**: A sequence of related API calls that represent a complete user journey

Example - User Onboarding Flow:

```python
class TestUserOnboardingFlow:
    """Test complete user onboarding workflow"""
    
    def test_complete_onboarding_flow(self):
        # Step 1: Request OTP
        otp_response = requests.post(f"{BASE_URL}/otp", json={
            "email": "newuser@example.com"
        })
        assert otp_response.status_code == 200
        
        # Step 2: Validate OTP (use test OTP code)
        validate_response = requests.post(f"{BASE_URL}/otp/validate", json={
            "email": "newuser@example.com",
            "otp": "123456"  # Test OTP
        })
        assert validate_response.status_code == 200
        tokens = validate_response.json()["data"]
        
        # Step 3: Access protected resource
        profile_response = requests.get(
            f"{BASE_URL}/users/profile",
            headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {tokens['accessToken']}"}
        )
        assert profile_response.status_code == 200
```

### Test Categories

**Integration Tests** (`test/tests/integration/`):

* Test API endpoints with real database
* Validate request/response contracts
* Test authentication and authorization
* Verify database state changes

**E2E Tests** (`test/tests/e2e/`):

* Test complete user workflows
* Combine UI and API interactions
* Validate business rules across layers

**Performance Tests** (`test/tests/performance/`):

* Load testing (currently out of scope)
* Stress testing (currently out of scope)
* Can be added in future iterations

## Testing Best Practices

### Do's

✅ **Test behavior, not implementation**: Focus on what the code does, not how
✅ **Use descriptive names**: Test names should explain what is being tested
✅ **Follow AAA pattern**: Arrange, Act, Assert for clarity
✅ **Mock external dependencies**: Keep tests fast and isolated
✅ **Test edge cases**: Null values, empty arrays, boundary conditions
✅ **Test error paths**: Exceptions, validation failures, network errors
✅ **Use test data builders**: Create reusable test data factories
✅ **Add `@DisplayName` to all Java tests**: Required for clarity

### Don'ts

❌ **Don't use `sleep()` or arbitrary delays**: Use proper async handling
❌ **Don't test implementation details**: Test public interfaces only
❌ **Don't share state between tests**: Each test should be independent
❌ **Don't skip tests**: Fix or remove them, don't leave them skipped
❌ **Don't test third-party code**: Trust that libraries work
❌ **Don't write overly complex tests**: If a test is hard to understand, simplify it

## Test Data Management

### Test Data Builders

```java
public class UserTestBuilder {
    private String email = "test@example.com";
    private String name = "Test User";
    private boolean isDeleted = false;
    
    public UserTestBuilder withEmail(String email) {
        this.email = email;
        return this;
    }
    
    public UserTestBuilder withName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        return this;
    }
    
    public UserTestBuilder deleted() {
        this.isDeleted = true;
        return this;
    }
    
    public User build() {
        return User.builder()
            .email(email)
            .name(name)
            .isDeleted(isDeleted)
            .createdAt(LocalDateTime.now())
            .build();
    }
}

// Usage
User user = new UserTestBuilder()
    .withEmail("custom@example.com")
    .withName("Custom Name")
    .build();
```

